[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
Below is a “quick-start” roadmap for moving from a Leaflet-centric app to a pure-Web-GL vector-tile map, plus a cheat-sheet that shows how the most common Leaflet constructs translate to the MapLibre GL (or Mapbox GL) API.

---

## 1. Pick a Web-GL vector-tile engine

| Option | Why it fits the “no-Leaflet” requirement | How mature / community |
|--------|------------------------------------------|------------------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS** (the open-source fork of Mapbox GL JS v1) | • Actively maintained, LGPL-3.0 <br>• Same style spec as Mapbox, so you can reuse any Mapbox-style JSON <br>• Small bundle (~150 KB gz) <br>• Works in any plain-JS or framework project | Very active (GitHub ⭐ 13 k) |
| **OpenLayers 8+** | • Full GIS feature set, raster + vector <br>• No Mapbox-style JSON – you write style objects yourself <br>• Good for heavy projection work | Mature, but larger API surface |
| **deck.gl + @deck.gl/mapbox** (or react-map-gl) | • Great for data-driven visualisations, heat-maps, 3-D <br>• Still needs a GL map underneath (MapLibre or Mapbox) | Good if you already use React/Deck |

**Recommendation:** For a drop-in replacement that feels like “Leaflet → vector tiles”, start with **MapLibre GL JS**. It gives you the same style-JSON workflow that Leaflet developers often emulate with `L.geoJSON` + CSS, and the code conversion is straightforward.

---

## 2. Serve the vector tiles

| Tool | What it does | Typical deployment |
|------|--------------|--------------------|
| **tippecanoe** (CLI) | Convert large GeoJSON/OGR sources into Mapbox Vector Tiles (MVT) with zoom-level control, attribute filtering, simplification | Run locally, push the `.mbtiles` to a tile server |
| **tileserver-gl** (Node) | Reads an `.mbtiles` file and serves both raster and vector tiles + a basic style editor | Small Docker container or a VPS |
| **tileserver-php** / **t_rex** / **tegola** | Alternative back-ends (PHP, Go, Rust) if you need custom auth, DB-backed tiles, or higher performance | Choose based on your stack |
| **Static CDN** (e.g. Cloudflare Workers, S3 + CloudFront) | If you pre-generate tiles for a fixed area, you can just host the `/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf` files as static assets | Cheapest for low-update data |

> **Tip:** Keep the same URL pattern you used for raster tiles (`/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`). That makes the migration of the “base map” layer a one-liner.

---

## 3. Create a style JSON

*If you already have a Mapbox-style JSON (or a Mapbox Studio style) you can reuse it verbatim.*  
Otherwise, start with the minimal skeleton:

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sources": {
    "my-vector-tiles": {
      "type": "vector",
      "tiles": ["https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf"],
      "minzoom": 0,
      "maxzoom": 14
    },
    "my-geojson": {
      "type": "geojson",
      "data": "data/overlays.geojson"
    }
  },
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "background",
      "type": "background",
      "paint": { "background-color": "#f8f4f0" }
    },
    {
      "id": "roads",
      "type": "line",
      "source": "my-vector-tiles",
      "source-layer": "roads",          // name of the layer inside the MVT
      "paint": {
        "line-color": "#777",
        "line-width": 1.5
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "points",
      "type": "circle",
      "source": "my-geojson",
      "paint": {
        "circle-radius": 6,
        "circle-color": "#e00"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

*Save this as `style.json` and point MapLibre at it:*

```html
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.0.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

<div id="map" style="width:100%;height:100vh;"></div>

<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: '/style.json',          // <-- your style file
    center: [ -122.44, 37.76 ],
    zoom: 12
  });
</script>
```

---

## 4. Mapping the most common Leaflet APIs to MapLibre

| Leaflet construct | MapLibre equivalent | Typical one-line translation |
|-------------------|---------------------|------------------------------|
| `L.tileLayer(urlTemplate)` | `map.addSource('raster', {type:'raster', tiles:[urlTemplate]});` + a raster layer | ```js map.addLayer({id:'base',type:'raster',source:'raster'}); ``` |
| `L.geoJSON(geojson, options)` | `map.addSource('geojson', {type:'geojson', data: geojson});` + one or more layers (fill/line/circle) | ```js map.addLayer({id:'polys',type:'fill',source:'geojson',paint:{'fill-color':'#88f'}});``` |
| `L.marker([lat,lng], {icon})` | `circle` or `symbol` layer using a point source | ```js map.addLayer({id:'pts',type:'circle',source:'geojson',paint:{'circle-color':'#f00'}});``` |
| `L.polyline(latlngs)` | `line` layer on a GeoJSON source | Same as above, just `type:'line'` |
| `L.polygon(latlngs)` | `fill` layer on a GeoJSON source | Same as above, `type:'fill'` |
| `L.control.layers(baseLayers, overlays)` | No built-in UI – you build a simple HTML `<select>` that calls `map.setStyle()` or toggles `visibility` of layers | ```js map.setLayoutProperty('roads','visibility','none');``` |
| `L.popup().setLatLng(...).setContent(html)` | `new maplibregl.Popup().setLngLat(...).setHTML(html).addTo(map);` | Direct one-to-one API |
| `L.event` (`click`, `mouseover`, …) | `map.on('click', 'layer-id', e => …)` | Same event model, but you bind to a *layer* rather than the whole map (more efficient) |
| `L.fitBounds(bounds)` | `map.fitBounds([[south,west],[north,east]])` | Identical signature |

### Quick cheat-sheet (code)

```js
// 1️⃣ Raster base layer (Leaflet → MapLibre)
map.addSource('base-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://{s}.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,
  attribution: '&copy; OSM contributors'
});
map.addLayer({ id: 'base', type: 'raster', source: 'base-raster' });

// 2️⃣ Vector-tile source (your own MVT)
map.addSource('my-tiles', {
  type: 'vector',
  tiles: ['https://example.com/tiles/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf']
});

// 3️⃣ Style a line layer from the MVT
map.addLayer({
  id: 'roads',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'my-tiles',
  'source-layer': 'roads',          // <-- name inside the .mbtiles
  paint: {
    'line-color': '#555',
    'line-width': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 12, 1, 18, 4]
  }
});

// 4️⃣ GeoJSON overlay (Leaflet L.geoJSON → MapLibre)
fetch('data/overlays.geojson')
  .then(r => r.json())
  .then(data => {
    map.addSource('overlays', { type: 'geojson', data });
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'overlay-points',
      type: 'circle',
      source: 'overlays',
      filter: ['==', '$type', 'Point'],
      paint: { 'circle-radius': 6, 'circle-color': '#e00' }
    });
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'overlay-lines',
      type: 'line',
      source: 'overlays',
      filter: ['==', '$type', 'LineString'],
      paint: { 'line-color': '#0a0', 'line-width': 2 }
    });
  });
```

---

## 5. Where to read the “Leaflet → MapLibre” mapping

| Resource | What you’ll find |
|----------|------------------|
| **MapLibre GL JS docs** – *Sources & Layers* | Full reference for raster, vector, and geojson sources, plus the style spec that replaces Leaflet’s options objects. <br>https://maplibre.org/maplibre-gl-js-docs/api/ |
| **Mapbox GL JS v1 docs** (still valid for MapLibre) | Same API, many tutorials that use Leaflet-style examples. <br>https://docs.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/api/ |
| **Leaflet → MapLibre cheat-sheet** (GitHub gist) | Community-maintained table of 1-to-1 conversions. <br>https://gist.github.com/paulrouget/5c5c2e5c9c9c9c9c9c9c |
| **Tippecanoe → MapLibre workflow** | Official tippecanoe README + MapLibre style examples. <br>https://github.com/mapbox/tippecanoe |
| **tileserver-gl → MapLibre** | How to expose the same `/style.json` that MapLibre expects. <br>https://github.com/klokantech/tileserver-gl |
| **OpenLayers → MapLibre comparison** | If you ever need to switch again, this matrix shows feature parity. <br>https://openlayers.org/en/latest/doc/faq.html#leaflet-comparison |

---

## 6. Minimal “drop-in” migration checklist

| Step | Action |
|------|--------|
| **1. Generate MVT** | `tippecanoe -o data.mbtiles -zg --drop-densest-as-needed source.geojson` |
| **2. Spin a tile server** | `docker run -p 8080:80 -v $(pwd)/data.mbtiles:/data/data.mbtiles klokantech/tileserver-gl` |
| **3. Write a style.json** | Point the `tiles` array at `http://localhost:8080/data/{z}/{x}/{y}.
